  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 AM EDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nStorms are well south of the area as they continue their push into \ncentral GA. As of the 03z SFC analysis, the cold front drapes across \nthe southern portion of the forecast area through southwest NC, \nnorth of Chattanooga, and NW into Middle TN south Nashville.\n\nDew points will be considerably less, with lower 70s still possibly \nhanging on today from about I-40 and south. Much lower and more \ncomfortable readings filter in later tonight and into tomorrow. \nEverywhere, with the exception of maybe a few isolated spots, will \nbe below 90 for highs tomorrow afternoon. Temperatures for the most \npart will remain steady through the forecast period, with bumps up \nor down a few degrees between the 80 and 90 degree mark (the \nvalley). What cannot be forgotten to mention is that we'll be dry \nunder high pressure sinking SSE-SE through the remainder of the work-\nweek. Unfortunately the much welcomed drier air will be replaced \nwith moist air again, when dew points are forecast to shoot up into \nearly Saturday. The low to mid 70s dews, especially across the \nvalley, will persist into early next week.\n\nA pattern change comes this weekend as we move into August, and \nappears will be a wet one. Persistent troughing over the NE will \nslowly rotate out just as a new shortwave begins to form over top \nthe SW US ridge. It'll cross into the Dakota's Thursday and then \nmake its dive SE through IA, IL, MO, and so on Friday. Around early \nor mid Saturday, it'll be knocking on our doorstep near western KY \nand TN. There are differences heading into the weekend if it will \nbecome a cut-off low, which could mean a near stationary system.\nNevertheless, there could be days with periods of rain Saturday into \nearly next week. The Day 5 ERO created by WPC yesterday afternoon, \nbrings a part of the slight risk for flash flooding into southern \nparts of the area Saturday into Sunday.",
